    "body": "View of the Admiral Bay project area. credit: Metalicity.\nBy: Northern Miner Staff November 25, 2026\nAustralia’s Massive Metals plans to raise up to $40 million (US$28 million) and go public in Canada through a reverse takeover of Argentum Silver (TSXV: ASL) to fund the first drilling since 2008 at its Admiral Bay zinc-lead-silver project in Western Australia.\nArgentum would issue 236.9 million shares to Massive Metals shareholders and seek to raise $30 million to $40 million in a brokered private placement before closing, the companies said Tuesday . The share exchange would give the Australian explorer a TSX Venture Exchange listing. Argentum expects shareholders to vote in January 2027.\n“We believe this approach has the potential to deliver higher-confidence drill targets and improve the likelihood of intersecting prospective mineralised horizons at depth,” Massive Metals Managing Director Michael Beilby said of combining historical seismic and drilling data with a new geophysical survey.\nPrevious attempt\nThe proposed deal renews Admiral Bay’s bid for Canadian financing. Former owner Metalicity pursued a listing for its Canadian subsidiary Kimberley Mining in 2018.\nAdmiral Bay lies 1,200 to 1,700 metres below surface, about 140 km south of Broome. Historical tests found elevated fluorine in lead and zinc concentrates, which could make them harder to sell.\nThe companies also disclosed that native title covers the project and no Indigenous land use agreement is in place.\nAdmiral Bay holds 77 million inferred tonnes grading 4.3% zinc, 3.4% lead and 24 grams silver per tonne for contained metal of 3.3 million tonnes of zinc, 2.6 million tonnes of lead and 59 million ounces of silver, according to an Oct. 6 resource. The estimate uses a US$82-per-tonne net smelter return cut-off.\nCanadian mining investor Eric Sprott held 62.2% of Argentum and 14.4% of Massive Metals through companies he controls when the companies agreed to the transaction. One of Sprott’s companies has lent Argentum $750,000 for corporate and transaction costs, according to Tuesday’s statement.\nThe takeover requires financing, exchange acceptance and approval from disinterested shareholders.\nArgentum last traded at 9.5¢ in Toronto before the stock was halted. That valued the company at $4.9 million.\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: Silver Zinc Zinc and Lead\nREGION: Australia, NZ & South Pacific Canada\nRepublish this article",
